# Single-Port RAM (8 x 16-bit) with a Self-Checking Testbench

An 8-location, 16-bit single-port RAM with one shared (inout) data bus and an asynchronous reset, plus a self-checking testbench. Simulated in Xilinx Vivado (behavioral simulation).

## How the RAM works

| wr_en | rd_en | What happens |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | 0 | Write: `data` is stored at address `add` on the rising clock edge |
| 0 | 1 | Read: the stored word appears on `data` straight away (no clock needed) |
| 0 | 0 | Nothing happens, `data` is high-Z |
| 1 | 1 | Nothing happens, `data` is high-Z |

`rst` is asynchronous and clears all 8 locations to 0.

## How the testbench works

- Keeps a reference copy of the memory (`mem_cpy`) and compares every read against it.
- Uses tasks `reset`, `write` and `read`, with clock-relative timing parameters (`cycle`, `thold`, `tsetup`).
- Test sequence: reset, read all 8 locations (all 0), write all 8 locations with random data, read them back, 20 random writes, read all 8 again, reset, read all 8 (all 0 again).
- Prints `ok: address N gave XXXX` for each good read, or a failure line with the expected value, and ends with `PASS` or `FAIL: n errors`.

## Results

- The good design ends with `PASS` at 1870 ns, and the `error` counter stays 0 for the whole run.
- Checking the testbench itself: in `ram.v`, change `wr_en&&!rd_en` to `!wr_en&&!rd_en`. Writes then never happen, and the testbench reports 16 errors (8 reads after the first writes and 8 after the random writes).

